;; Provisioning, for an appliance with no login prompt.
;;
;; The appliance formats its state partition if blank, generates a keypair if
;; it has none, brings up wg0 and prints the public key on the console.
;; Nothing can connect until a peer is added, which is the one step a
;; deployment must do and the one step that cannot be done in advance.
;;
;; Printing the key on the console is the only way this appliance says who it
;; is, and that is only safe because nothing else on the console answers. So
;; the feature requires no-console-login rather than providing it: this pack
;; used to mask the getty units itself, which made the one image composing it
;; locked and left every other locked image offering a prompt. The masks now
;; live with profile:locked, which is the only thing that provides the
;; capability, and composing this feature under a profile that offers a login
;; is a check failure rather than a quiet regression.

(fragment feature:wireguard-appliance

  (doc "First-boot WireGuard provisioning on a state partition, for an image with no console login")

  (requires
    (capability mmu)
    (capability systemd-init)
    (capability no-console-login))

  (buildroot
    ;; blkid and mountpoint, to tell a blank disk from a provisioned one and
    ;; an already-mounted directory from an empty one. Busybox has both but
    ;; not reliably enough to depend on across configurations.
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX_BINARIES)

    ;; mkfs.ext4 for the state partition on first boot.
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_E2FSPROGS)

    ;; ip(8). wg-quick is deliberately not used: it is a bash script, and
    ;; bash is a shell this appliance has no reason to carry.
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_IPROUTE2)

    (path-append BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY "files")))
